   "source": [
    "{\n",
    " \"cells\": [\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"b4a7a5ed\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# Apache Hive on Google Colab — Apache Log File Analysis\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### Big Data Analytics · Session 7 · Hands-on Session: Coding\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Learning outcome:** *Ability to perform analysis using Hive.*\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This notebook installs **real Apache Hive** in your Colab session and analyses a web-server log file\\n\",\n",
    "    \"using **HiveQL** — the SQL-like language Hive uses.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Why Colab:** Colab runs Linux in the cloud, so nothing installs on your own laptop.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Mac and Windows students get an identical environment. You only need a browser.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### How to run\\n\",\n",
    "    \"1. **Runtime → Run all** (or run cells top to bottom).\\n\",\n",
    "    \"2. Section 1 takes **3–6 minutes** — it downloads Hadoop and Hive. Run it once and let it finish.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"3. After that, every query returns in seconds.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"> ⚠️ **Colab sessions are temporary.** If the runtime disconnects, re-run Section 1.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"> Nothing is saved to your Google Drive unless you deliberately save it.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"d43e6cd0\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## Section 1 · Install Hive (run once per session)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Hive is not a standalone program — it sits on top of Hadoop and needs **Java**.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"So we install three things in order: **Java → Hadoop → Hive**.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"We pin exact versions and download from `archive.apache.org`, which keeps every past release\\n\",\n",
    "    \"permanently. (The `downloads.apache.org` mirror deletes old versions, which is the usual reason\\n\",\n",
    "    \"a tutorial's link suddenly dies mid-semester.)\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"8549f517\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### 1.1 · Java 8\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Hive 3.x is built for **Java 8**. Newer Java (17, 21) causes hard-to-read reflection errors,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"so we install Java 8 explicitly rather than trusting whatever Colab ships with.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"f710ffcd\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"%%bash\\n\",\n",
    "    \"apt-get -qq update > /dev/null 2>&1\\n\",\n",
    "    \"apt-get -qq install -y openjdk-8-jdk-headless > /dev/null 2>&1\\n\",\n",
    "    \"echo \\\"Java 8 installed:\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ls -d /usr/lib/jvm/java-8-openjdk-* 2>/dev/null || echo \\\"  !! not found — see Troubleshooting at the end\\\"\\n\"\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"5973451b\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### 1.2 · Download Hadoop and Hive\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Hadoop is ~700 MB, so this is the slow cell. Colab's network is fast — expect a few minutes.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Each download tries a backup mirror if the first fails.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"d1a3500e\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"import os, subprocess, sys\\n\",\n",
    "    \"from pathlib import Path\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"BASE = Path(\\\"/content\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"HADOOP_VER, HIVE_VER = \\\"3.3.6\\\", \\\"3.1.3\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"HADOOP_URLS = [\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    f\\\"https://archive.apache.org/dist/hadoop/common/hadoop-{HADOOP_VER}/hadoop-{HADOOP_VER}.tar.gz\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    f\\\"https://downloads.apache.org/hadoop/common/hadoop-{HADOOP_VER}/hadoop-{HADOOP_VER}.tar.gz\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"HIVE_URLS = [\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    f\\\"https://archive.apache.org/dist/hive/hive-{HIVE_VER}/apache-hive-{HIVE_VER}-bin.tar.gz\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    f\\\"https://downloads.apache.org/hive/hive-{HIVE_VER}/apache-hive-{HIVE_VER}-bin.tar.gz\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"def fetch(urls, out):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"Try each mirror until one works.\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    out = BASE / out\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    if out.exists():\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        print(f\\\"  already downloaded: {out.name}\\\"); return out\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    for u in urls:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        print(f\\\"  trying {u.split('/')[2]} ...\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        r = subprocess.run([\\\"wget\\\", \\\"-q\\\", \\\"--show-progress\\\", \\\"-O\\\", str(out), u])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        if r.returncode == 0 and out.stat().st_size > 1_000_000:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"            print(f\\\"  OK -> {out.name} ({out.stat().st_size/1e6:.0f} MB)\\\"); return out\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        out.unlink(missing_ok=True)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    raise RuntimeError(f\\\"All mirrors failed for {out.name}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"os.chdir(BASE)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"Hadoop:\\\"); h_tar = fetch(HADOOP_URLS, f\\\"hadoop-{HADOOP_VER}.tar.gz\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"Hive:\\\");   v_tar = fetch(HIVE_URLS,   f\\\"apache-hive-{HIVE_VER}-bin.tar.gz\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"\\\\nExtracting (about a minute) ...\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"if not (BASE / f\\\"hadoop-{HADOOP_VER}\\\").exists():\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    subprocess.run([\\\"tar\\\", \\\"-xzf\\\", str(h_tar), \\\"-C\\\", str(BASE)], check=True)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"if not (BASE / f\\\"apache-hive-{HIVE_VER}-bin\\\").exists():\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    subprocess.run([\\\"tar\\\", \\\"-xzf\\\", str(v_tar), \\\"-C\\\", str(BASE)], check=True)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"Done.\\\")\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"0b733ebf\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### 1.3 · Environment variables + the two classic fixes\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Two well-known problems are handled here **before** they can bite:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Problem | Symptom you would otherwise see | Fix applied |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"|---|---|---|\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| **Guava version clash** | `NoSuchMethodError: com.google.common.base.Preconditions.checkArgument` | Delete Hive's old `guava-19` jar, copy Hadoop's newer one in |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| **Broken config template** | `Illegal character entity: expansion character (code 0x8 ...)` | Write our own clean minimal `hive-site.xml` instead of copying Hive's template |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"We also point Hive at the **local filesystem** (`file:///`) so no HDFS or cluster is needed.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"8c929004\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"import glob, shutil, textwrap\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# --- JAVA_HOME: find it rather than hard-coding (survives Colab image changes)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"java_home = sorted(glob.glob(\\\"/usr/lib/jvm/java-8-openjdk-*\\\"))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"assert java_home, \\\"Java 8 not found — re-run cell 1.1\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"os.environ[\\\"JAVA_HOME\\\"]   = java_home[0]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"os.environ[\\\"HADOOP_HOME\\\"] = str(BASE / f\\\"hadoop-{HADOOP_VER}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"os.environ[\\\"HIVE_HOME\\\"]   = str(BASE / f\\\"apache-hive-{HIVE_VER}-bin\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"os.environ[\\\"PATH\\\"] = (f\\\"{os.environ['HIVE_HOME']}/bin:{os.environ['HADOOP_HOME']}/bin:\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                      f\\\"{os.environ['JAVA_HOME']}/bin:\\\" + os.environ[\\\"PATH\\\"])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"os.environ[\\\"HADOOP_CLIENT_OPTS\\\"] = \\\"-Xmx1g\\\"     # keep Colab RAM happy\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# --- FIX 1: guava clash (match by glob so exact versions don't matter)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"hive_guava   = glob.glob(f\\\"{os.environ['HIVE_HOME']}/lib/guava-*.jar\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"hadoop_guava = glob.glob(f\\\"{os.environ['HADOOP_HOME']}/share/hadoop/hdfs/lib/guava-*.jar\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"if hive_guava and hadoop_guava:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    for g in hive_guava:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        os.remove(g)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    shutil.copy(hadoop_guava[0], f\\\"{os.environ['HIVE_HOME']}/lib/\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    print(\\\"Guava fix applied:\\\", os.path.basename(hadoop_guava[0]))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# --- FIX 2: our own clean hive-site.xml (avoids the corrupt shipped template)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"WAREHOUSE = BASE / \\\"hive_warehouse\\\"; WAREHOUSE.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SCRATCH   = BASE / \\\"hive_scratch\\\";   SCRATCH.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"def prop(n, v):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    return f\\\"  <property><name>{n}</name><value>{v}</value></property>\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"hive_site = \\\"<?xml version=\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\"?>\\\\n<configuration>\\\\n\\\" + \\\"\\\\n\\\".join([\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"javax.jdo.option.ConnectionURL\\\", \\\"jdbc:derby:;databaseName=/content/metastore_db;create=true\\\"),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"javax.jdo.option.ConnectionDriverName\\\", \\\"org.apache.derby.jdbc.EmbeddedDriver\\\"),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"hive.metastore.warehouse.dir\\\", f\\\"file://{WAREHOUSE}\\\"),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"hive.exec.scratchdir\\\", str(SCRATCH)),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"fs.defaultFS\\\", \\\"file:///\\\"),                    # no HDFS needed\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"mapreduce.framework.name\\\", \\\"local\\\"),           # no YARN needed\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"hive.execution.engine\\\", \\\"mr\\\"),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"hive.metastore.schema.verification\\\", \\\"false\\\"),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"hive.metastore.event.db.notification.api.auth\\\", \\\"false\\\"),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    prop(\\\"hive.cli.print.header\\\", \\\"true\\\"),               # show column names in output\\n\",\n",
    "    \"]) + \\\"\\\\n</configuration>\\\\n\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Path(f\\\"{os.environ['HIVE_HOME']}/conf/hive-site.xml\\\").write_text(hive_site)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"hive-site.xml written\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"JAVA_HOME  :\\\", os.environ[\\\"JAVA_HOME\\\"])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"HIVE_HOME  :\\\", os.environ[\\\"HIVE_HOME\\\"])\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"f4d2b895\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### 1.4 · Initialise the metastore\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Hive keeps its **table definitions** (names, columns, types) in a small database called the\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**metastore**. We use the built-in Derby database — fine for one user.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"> **Concept worth saying out loud:** the metastore holds *only the description* of the table.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"> The actual data stays in files. That's why Hive is called **schema-on-read** — you attach a\\n\",\n",
    "    \"> table shape to files that already exist, instead of loading data into a database first.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"31c4ae45\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"%%bash\\n\",\n",
    "    \"cd /content\\n\",\n",
    "    \"rm -rf metastore_db          # clean slate; safe to re-run this cell\\n\",\n",
    "    \"$HIVE_HOME/bin/schematool -dbType derby -initSchema 2>&1 | tail -6\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"9b42839f\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### 1.5 · Check it works\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"If you see `OK` and a list containing `default`, Hive is running.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"a25e3fe0\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"%%bash\\n\",\n",
    "    \"cd /content\\n\",\n",
    "    \"hive -e \\\"SHOW DATABASES;\\\" 2>/dev/null\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"f87c205d\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## Section 2 · The data — an Apache web server log\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Every web server writes a **log file**: one line per request. This is the classic\\n\",\n",
    "    \"*semi-structured* data from Session 1 — it has structure, but it isn't a neat table.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"We generate a realistic log in **Apache Combined Log Format** so the notebook is self-contained.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"A single line looks like this:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"```\\n\",\n",
    "    \"203.0.113.5 - - [11/Oct/2025:13:55:36 +0530] \\\"GET /product/shoes HTTP/1.1\\\" 200 5324 \\\"-\\\" \\\"Mozilla/5.0\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"```\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Reading left to right: **who** asked (IP), **when**, **what** they asked for (method + page),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**what happened** (status code — 200 is fine, 404 is missing, 500 is broken), and **how many bytes** came back.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"5f42f3bc\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"import random\\n\",\n",
    "    \"from datetime import datetime, timedelta\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"random.seed(42)                          # everyone gets identical data\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LOG_PATH = BASE / \\\"logdata\\\" / \\\"access_log\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LOG_PATH.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"PAGES = [\\\"/\\\", \\\"/product/shoes\\\", \\\"/product/laptop\\\", \\\"/cart\\\", \\\"/checkout\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"         \\\"/search\\\", \\\"/offers\\\", \\\"/account\\\", \\\"/product/phone\\\", \\\"/help\\\"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"AGENTS = [\\\"Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0)\\\", \\\"Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh)\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"          \\\"Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone)\\\", \\\"Mozilla/5.0 (Android)\\\"]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"start = datetime(2025, 10, 11, 0, 0, 0)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"lines = []\\n\",\n",
    "    \"for i in range(200_000):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ip     = f\\\"203.0.113.{random.randint(1,250)}\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ts     = start + timedelta(seconds=random.randint(0, 86_399))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    page   = random.choices(PAGES, weights=[30,14,12,10,7,9,6,5,4,3])[0]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    method = random.choices([\\\"GET\\\",\\\"POST\\\"], weights=[92,8])[0]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    status = random.choices([200,404,500,302], weights=[90,5,2,3])[0]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    size   = random.randint(400, 9000) if status == 200 else random.randint(100, 700)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    stamp  = ts.strftime(\\\"%d/%b/%Y:%H:%M:%S +0530\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    lines.append(f'{ip} - - [{stamp}] \\\"{method} {page} HTTP/1.1\\\" {status} {size} \\\"-\\\" \\\"{random.choice(AGENTS)}\\\"')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LOG_PATH.write_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(lines) + \\\"\\\\n\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Wrote {len(lines):,} log lines to {LOG_PATH}\\\\n\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"First 3 lines:\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"for l in lines[:3]:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    print(\\\"  \\\", l)\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"26c213c5\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## Section 3 · A helper so queries are easy to read\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Hive prints a lot of technical logging around each answer. This helper runs a HiveQL query and\\n\",\n",
    "    \"shows **only the result**, so the output stays readable in class.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"You still write ordinary HiveQL — the helper just tidies the display.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"6d14aaa2\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": \"import subprocess, textwrap, tempfile, os\\n\\ndef hql(query, show_query=True):\\n    \\\"Run a HiveQL query and print just the results.\\\"\\n    if show_query:\\n        print(\\\"HiveQL:\\\", textwrap.dedent(query).strip(), \\\"\\\\n\\\" + \\\"-\\\"*60)\\n    # Strip a trailing ';' — this Hive build fails to strip it itself for\\n    # multi-line statements (\\\"extraneous input ';' expecting EOF\\\"), so we\\n    # never hand it one.\\n    clean_query = query.strip()\\n    if clean_query.endswith(\\\";\\\"):\\n        clean_query = clean_query[:-1]\\n    with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(mode=\\\"w\\\", suffix=\\\".hql\\\", dir=\\\"/content\\\",\\n                                      delete=False) as f:\\n        f.write(clean_query)\\n        script_path = f.name\\n    try:\\n        r = subprocess.run([\\\"hive\\\", \\\"-f\\\", script_path], cwd=\\\"/content\\\",\\n                           capture_output=True, text=True)\\n    finally:\\n        os.remove(script_path)\\n    if r.stdout.strip():\\n        print(r.stdout.strip())\\n    if r.returncode != 0:\\n        print(\\\"\\\\n--- Hive reported a problem ---\\\")\\n        print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(r.stderr.strip().splitlines()[-15:]))\\n    return r.stdout\\n\\nprint(\\\"Helper ready. Use:  hql(\\\\\\\"SELECT ...\\\\\\\")\\\")\\n\"\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c3f4d103\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## Section 4 · Create a Hive table over the log file\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Here is the idea that makes Hive click:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"> **We are not loading the data anywhere. We are laying a table shape *on top of* a file that already exists.**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The log lines aren't comma-separated, so we describe each line with a **pattern** (a regular\\n\",\n",
    "    \"expression) that tells Hive which piece is the IP, which is the timestamp, and so on.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"That's the `RegexSerDe` below — *SerDe* just means \\\"how to read/write these rows.\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"You never need to write that pattern from scratch. Read it as: *\\\"this column comes from that\\n\",\n",
    "    \"part of the line.\\\"*\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"e41dba81\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": \"hql(\\\"DROP TABLE IF EXISTS access_logs;\\\", show_query=False)\\n\\ncreate_table = r'''\\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE access_logs (\\n  ip        STRING,\\n  log_time  STRING,\\n  method    STRING,\\n  page      STRING,\\n  status    INT,\\n  bytes     INT,\\n  agent     STRING\\n)\\nROW FORMAT SERDE 'org.apache.hadoop.hive.serde2.RegexSerDe'\\nWITH SERDEPROPERTIES (\\n  \\\"input.regex\\\" = '^(\\\\\\\\S+) \\\\\\\\S+ \\\\\\\\S+ \\\\\\\\[([^\\\\\\\\]]+)\\\\\\\\] \\\"(\\\\\\\\S+) (\\\\\\\\S+) [^\\\"]*\\\" (\\\\\\\\d+) (\\\\\\\\d+) \\\"[^\\\"]*\\\" \\\"([^\\\"]*)\\\"$'\\n)\\nSTORED AS TEXTFILE\\nLOCATION '/content/logdata';\\n'''\\nhql(create_table, show_query=False)\\nprint(\\\"Table created.\\\\n\\\")\\nhql(\\\"DESCRIBE access_logs;\\\")\\n\"\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"a43a429f\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"**`EXTERNAL` matters.** It tells Hive: *the file is not mine — I'm only describing it.*\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Drop the table later and the log file stays untouched. That is the normal, safe way to point\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Hive at data your organisation already has.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"1e36dc33\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"hql(\\\"SELECT * FROM access_logs LIMIT 5;\\\")\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"6cbf7272\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## Section 5 · Answer real questions with HiveQL\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This is the payoff: **these are ordinary SQL queries.** If you have written SQL before, you\\n\",\n",
    "    \"already know Hive. If you haven't, read each one as an English sentence.\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c56e10f6\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Q1 · How much traffic did we get?\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"99ffa442\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"hql(\\\"SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_requests FROM access_logs;\\\")\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"107d789c\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Q2 · Which pages are most popular?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"*\\\"Count the requests for each page, show the busiest first.\\\"*\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"755dcce2\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"hql('''\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT page, COUNT(*) AS hits\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"GROUP BY page\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ORDER BY hits DESC\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LIMIT 10;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"''')\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"782d160c\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Q3 · Is anything broken?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Status **404** = page missing, **500** = server error. A business question hides in here:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"*are customers hitting errors on pages that matter?*\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"ac98bf71\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"hql('''\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS requests\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"GROUP BY status\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ORDER BY requests DESC;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"''')\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"128463d9\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Q4 · When is the site busiest?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The timestamp looks like `11/Oct/2025:13:55:36 +0530`. The hour sits at characters 13–14,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"so we cut it out with `SUBSTR`. This is very common with log data — the raw field needs a\\n\",\n",
    "    \"little shaping before it becomes useful.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"f39cecca\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"hql('''\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT SUBSTR(log_time, 13, 2) AS hour_of_day,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"       COUNT(*) AS requests\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"GROUP BY SUBSTR(log_time, 13, 2)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ORDER BY hour_of_day;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"''')\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"3be8ef76\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Q5 · Which pages produce the most errors?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Two ideas combined: **filter** to error rows, then **group**. This is exactly the\\n\",\n",
    "    \"*filter → group → total → sort* rhythm you already use in Excel.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"ac947e0b\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"hql('''\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT page,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"       COUNT(*) AS error_hits\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"WHERE status IN (404, 500)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"GROUP BY page\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ORDER BY error_hits DESC\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LIMIT 5;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"''')\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"ef4300e9\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Q6 · How much data did we serve?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"`SUM` on the bytes column, converted to megabytes — the kind of number an infrastructure\\n\",\n",
    "    \"budget conversation actually needs.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"554e54d2\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"hql('''\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT ROUND(SUM(bytes)/1024/1024, 2) AS total_megabytes,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"       ROUND(AVG(bytes), 0)           AS avg_bytes_per_request\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"WHERE status = 200;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"''')\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"14c84a0b\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"---\\n## Section 5b · Turn results into charts\\n\\nA table of numbers is correct; a chart is what a manager actually looks at. Nothing new\\nconceptually — we run the **same HiveQL**, just capture the result into a `pandas`\\nDataFrame instead of only printing it, then hand that to `matplotlib`.\\n\\n`hql()` already tidies Hive's console output. `hql_df()` below does one more step: it\\nparses that tab-separated output into a DataFrame you can plot, `.describe()`, or export.\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {}\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"97327662\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"import pandas as pd\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\\n# Palette matched to the session slides — one accent per meaning, not per bar.\\nBLUE, GOLD, GREEN, RED, INK, GRID = \\\"#3BA5E7\\\", \\\"#F0B44D\\\", \\\"#39B87F\\\", \\\"#E35B5B\\\", \\\"#1f2933\\\", \\\"#e3e7eb\\\"\\nplt.rcParams.update({\\n    \\\"figure.facecolor\\\": \\\"white\\\", \\\"axes.facecolor\\\": \\\"white\\\",\\n    \\\"axes.edgecolor\\\": GRID, \\\"axes.grid\\\": True, \\\"grid.color\\\": GRID, \\\"grid.linewidth\\\": .8,\\n    \\\"axes.spines.top\\\": False, \\\"axes.spines.right\\\": False, \\\"axes.spines.left\\\": False,\\n    \\\"font.size\\\": 11, \\\"text.color\\\": INK, \\\"axes.labelcolor\\\": INK,\\n    \\\"xtick.color\\\": INK, \\\"ytick.color\\\": INK,\\n})\\n\\ndef hql_df(query):\\n    \\\"Run a HiveQL query and return the result as a pandas DataFrame.\\\"\\n    out = hql(query, show_query=False)\\n    rows = [r.split(\\\"\\\\t\\\") for r in out.splitlines() if r.strip()]\\n    header = [c.split(\\\".\\\")[-1] for c in rows[0]]     # drop Hive's \\\"table.\\\" prefix\\n    df = pd.DataFrame(rows[1:], columns=header)\\n    for col in df.columns:\\n        df[col] = pd.to_numeric(df[col], errors=\\\"ignore\\\")\\n    return df\\n\\nprint(\\\"hql_df ready. Use:  df = hql_df(\\\\\\\"SELECT ...\\\\\\\")\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"outputs\": []\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"d2d2565a\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"### Chart 1 · Most popular pages\\nSame query as Q2, plotted as a horizontal bar — one hue, since these bars are all the\\nsame kind of thing (page traffic), not different categories.\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {}\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"dd65b32b\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"pages = hql_df('''\\nSELECT page, COUNT(*) AS hits\\nFROM access_logs\\nGROUP BY page\\nORDER BY hits DESC\\nLIMIT 10;\\n''').sort_values(\\\"hits\\\")\\n\\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4.5))\\nax.barh(pages[\\\"page\\\"], pages[\\\"hits\\\"], color=BLUE, height=.62)\\nax.set_xlabel(\\\"requests\\\"); ax.set_title(\\\"Top 10 pages by traffic\\\", loc=\\\"left\\\", fontweight=\\\"bold\\\")\\nax.grid(axis=\\\"y\\\", visible=False)\\nfor y, v in enumerate(pages[\\\"hits\\\"]):\\n    ax.text(v, y, f\\\"  {v:,}\\\", va=\\\"center\\\", fontsize=9, color=INK)\\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\\n\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"outputs\": []\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"2095ec2d\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"<details>\\n<summary><b>What this chart tells you</b></summary>\\n\\n- **Why a horizontal bar, not vertical:** page names are long (`/product/laptop`) — horizontal\\n  bars leave room to read them without rotating text.\\n- **Why sorted, not alphabetical:** `sort_values(\\\"hits\\\")` puts the busiest page at the *top*\\n  (matplotlib draws bars bottom-up, so the largest value goes last). Sorting by rank is what\\n  makes \\\"busiest first\\\" visible at a glance instead of requiring the reader to scan numbers.\\n- **Why one color:** every bar answers the same question — \\\"how much traffic does this page\\n  get\\\" — so there's nothing for color to distinguish. A different hue per bar would imply the\\n  pages are different *kinds* of thing, which they aren't.\\n- **Reading it:** `/` dominates because it's the landing page everyone hits first. The real\\n  signal is further down — `/cart` and `/checkout` are high-traffic *and* important to\\n  business revenue, which is why Chart 4 checks whether they're also breaking.\\n</details>\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {}\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"b17c3442\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"### Chart 2 · Status code mix\\nHere color *does* carry meaning, so each bar gets the status color from the slides:\\ngreen = OK, gold = redirect, red = error. That is a **status palette**, not a\\ncategorical one — it's reused consistently, not picked per chart.\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {}\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"2ad3768f\",\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"status = hql_df('''\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS requests\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"GROUP BY status\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ORDER BY requests DESC;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"''')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"STATUS_COLOR = {200: GREEN, 302: GOLD, 404: RED, 500: RED}\\n\",\n",
    "    \"labels = status[\\\"status\\\"].astype(int).astype(str)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"colors = [STATUS_COLOR.get(s, BLUE) for s in status[\\\"status\\\"].astype(int)]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 4))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"bars = ax.bar(labels, status[\\\"requests\\\"], color=colors, width=.6)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.set_ylabel(\\\"requests\\\"); ax.set_xlabel(\\\"HTTP status\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.set_title(\\\"Requests by status code\\\", loc=\\\"left\\\", fontweight=\\\"bold\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"for b, v in zip(bars, status[\\\"requests\\\"]):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ax.text(b.get_x() + b.get_width()/2, v, f\\\"{v:,}\\\", ha=\\\"center\\\", va=\\\"bottom\\\", fontsize=9)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"error_share = status.loc[status[\\\"status\\\"].astype(int).isin([404, 500]), \\\"requests\\\"].sum() / status[\\\"requests\\\"].sum()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Error share (404+500): {error_share:.1%}\\\")\\n\"\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"outputs\": []\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"77386493\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"<details>\\n<summary><b>What this chart tells you</b></summary>\\n\\n- **Why a vertical bar here, not horizontal:** there are only 4 categories with short labels\\n  (`200`, `302`, `404`, `500`) — no room problem, and a vertical bar reads left-to-right in\\n  the natural \\\"best to worst\\\" order Hive already sorted them into.\\n- **Why color carries meaning here but not in Chart 1:** in Chart 1 every bar meant the same\\n  thing (traffic). Here the bars are genuinely different *states* — success, redirect, error —\\n  so color is doing real work: green/gold/red is a **status palette**, matching the same\\n  meaning used on the slides, not a fresh color choice per chart.\\n- **The `error_share` line:** the chart shows counts; the printed percentage is the number a\\n  manager actually reacts to. ~7% failing means roughly **1 in 14 visitors hits a wall** —\\n  easy to miss by eye in a bar chart, easy to act on as a single number.\\n- **Reading it:** 200 (OK) will always be the tallest bar on a healthy site — that's expected,\\n  not the story. The story is whether 404+500 together are small and flat over time, or growing.\\n</details>\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {}\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"b44ae957\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"### Chart 3 · Traffic by hour of day\\nA line is the right form here — 24 ordered hours, one continuous measure. Watch for the\\nevening ridge called out on the slides.\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {}\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"36ea0f8a\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"hourly = hql_df('''\\nSELECT SUBSTR(log_time, 13, 2) AS hour_of_day,\\n       COUNT(*) AS requests\\nFROM access_logs\\nGROUP BY SUBSTR(log_time, 13, 2)\\nORDER BY hour_of_day;\\n''')\\nhourly[\\\"hour_of_day\\\"] = hourly[\\\"hour_of_day\\\"].astype(int)\\npeak = hourly.loc[hourly[\\\"requests\\\"].idxmax()]\\n\\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 4))\\nax.plot(hourly[\\\"hour_of_day\\\"], hourly[\\\"requests\\\"], color=BLUE, linewidth=2)\\nax.scatter([peak[\\\"hour_of_day\\\"]], [peak[\\\"requests\\\"]], color=GOLD, zorder=3, s=60)\\nax.annotate(f\\\"peak {int(peak['hour_of_day']):02d}:00\\\", (peak[\\\"hour_of_day\\\"], peak[\\\"requests\\\"]),\\n            textcoords=\\\"offset points\\\", xytext=(0, 10), ha=\\\"center\\\", fontweight=\\\"bold\\\")\\nax.set_xlabel(\\\"hour of day\\\"); ax.set_ylabel(\\\"requests\\\")\\nax.set_xticks(range(0, 24, 2))\\nax.set_title(\\\"Requests by hour\\\", loc=\\\"left\\\", fontweight=\\\"bold\\\")\\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\\n\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"outputs\": []\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c36a33c3\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"<details>\\n<summary><b>What this chart tells you</b></summary>\\n\\n- **Why a line, not bars:** the 24 hours form one continuous sequence (`00:00 → 23:00`), and a\\n  line is the form built for \\\"how does this change as we move along an axis.\\\" Bars would work\\n  too, but a line makes the *shape* of the day — rise, plateau, dip — easier to see than 24\\n  separate rectangles would.\\n- **Why annotate only the peak:** labeling all 24 points would bury the one number that\\n  matters. A single direct label at the peak answers \\\"when are we busiest?\\\" without making the\\n  reader hunt for it — the same \\\"selective label, not one on every point\\\" rule as the slides.\\n- **`idxmax()`:** finds the row with the largest `requests` value automatically, so the peak\\n  annotation is correct even if you regenerate the log with different random data.\\n- **Reading it:** a flat line with one ridge (as here) is a **staffing** conversation — schedule\\n  more support around the peak hour. A line with a sharp single spike instead would suggest a\\n  scheduled event (a sale, a push notification) rather than steady daily behaviour.\\n</details>\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {}\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"3b8152e8\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"### Chart 4 · Where errors concentrate\\nRed throughout — this whole chart is \\\"trouble,\\\" so one color, not a rainbow per page.\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {}\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"f4be5c27\",\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"errors = hql_df('''\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT page, COUNT(*) AS error_hits\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"WHERE status IN (404, 500)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"GROUP BY page\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ORDER BY error_hits DESC\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LIMIT 5;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"''').sort_values(\\\"error_hits\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 3.5))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.barh(errors[\\\"page\\\"], errors[\\\"error_hits\\\"], color=RED, height=.55)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.set_xlabel(\\\"errors (404 + 500)\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.set_title(\\\"Top 5 pages by error count\\\", loc=\\\"left\\\", fontweight=\\\"bold\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.grid(axis=\\\"y\\\", visible=False)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"for y, v in enumerate(errors[\\\"error_hits\\\"]):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ax.text(v, y, f\\\"  {v:,}\\\", va=\\\"center\\\", fontsize=9, color=INK)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\\n\"\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"outputs\": []\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"8b67771b\",\n",
    "   \"source\": \"<details>\\n<summary><b>What this chart tells you</b></summary>\\n\\n- **Why red for every bar:** this chart only exists to answer \\\"where is it broken\\\" — every bar\\n  is bad news by definition, so red is the whole chart's message, not a per-bar distinction.\\n  Compare this to Chart 2, where red was *one* state among several.\\n- **Why `LIMIT 5`, not all 10 pages:** past the top handful, error counts are noise you'd act on\\n  differently (or not at all). Showing only the pages worth discussing keeps the chart a\\n  decision tool instead of a full report.\\n- **Reading it together with Chart 1:** a page appearing high on *both* charts (traffic **and**\\n  errors) is the priority — it's not just broken, it's broken in front of a lot of people. A\\n  page with few visits but a high error *rate* wouldn't necessarily show up here at all, because\\n  this chart ranks by error **count**, not error **rate** (`errors / visits`). That's a natural\\n  Exercise: divide this by each page's total hits from Chart 1's query to rank by rate instead.\\n- **Decision:** on this data, `/cart` has the most errors — a broken cart is an abandoned\\n  purchase, which is exactly why it also gets called out as a callout on the slides.\\n</details>\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {}\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"187ac86d\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## Section 6 · Your turn\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Change **one thing at a time** and re-run. You are not expected to write these from a blank page.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Exercise 1** — Q2 shows the top 10 pages. Change it to show the top **3**.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Exercise 2** — Find the **busiest visitors**: group by `ip` instead of `page`.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Exercise 3** — Q5 looks at 404 and 500 together. Change it to show **only 404s**.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Exercise 4** — Count how many requests came from **iPhone** users.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"*Hint:* `WHERE agent LIKE '%iPhone%'`\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Exercise 5 (interpretation, no code)** — Look at your Q3 output. If 5% of all requests are\\n\",\n",
    "    \"404s, what would you tell the e-commerce team to do on Monday morning?\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"72d85444\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# Exercise 1 — top 3 pages\\n\",\n",
    "    \"hql('''\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT page, COUNT(*) AS hits\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"GROUP BY page\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ORDER BY hits DESC\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LIMIT 10;          -- <-- change 10 to 3\\n\",\n",
    "    \"''')\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"f3b1453c\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# Exercise 2 — busiest visitors (fill in the blank)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"hql('''\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT ____ , COUNT(*) AS hits\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"GROUP BY ____\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ORDER BY hits DESC\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LIMIT 5;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"''')\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"f1887df8\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"<details><summary><b>Show solutions</b></summary>\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"```sql\\n\",\n",
    "    \"-- Ex 1: change LIMIT 10 to LIMIT 3\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"-- Ex 2:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT ip, COUNT(*) AS hits FROM access_logs GROUP BY ip ORDER BY hits DESC LIMIT 5;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"-- Ex 3:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT page, COUNT(*) AS error_hits FROM access_logs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"WHERE status = 404 GROUP BY page ORDER BY error_hits DESC LIMIT 5;\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"-- Ex 4:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"SELECT COUNT(*) AS iphone_requests FROM access_logs WHERE agent LIKE '%iPhone%';\\n\",\n",
    "    \"```\\n\",\n",
    "    \"</details>\\n\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"105e15c4\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": \"---\\n## Section 7 · What you just did\\n\\n- Installed **real Apache Hive** and pointed it at a raw web-server log.\\n- Used **schema-on-read**: you described a table *over files that already existed* — you never\\n  loaded data into a database.\\n- Answered six genuine business questions in **plain SQL**: traffic, popular pages, errors,\\n  peak hours, error hotspots, bandwidth.\\n- Turned four of those results into **charts** — pulling a Hive query straight into `pandas`\\n  and `matplotlib`, so the same query that answers the question also produces the visual.\\n\\n**The idea to carry forward:** Hive lets you ask SQL questions of files that are far too large\\nfor a spreadsheet — without learning a new language. The queries you wrote today would run\\nunchanged over billions of log lines on a real cluster. *Only the scale changes.*\"\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"a63e95f1\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## Troubleshooting\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**\\\"Java 8 not found\\\"** — Colab's base image changed. Try:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"`!apt-get install -y openjdk-11-jdk-headless` then edit the glob in 1.3 to `java-11-openjdk-*`.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Download fails / all mirrors failed** — Check the version still exists at\\n\",\n",
    "    \"[archive.apache.org/dist/hive](https://archive.apache.org/dist/hive/) and\\n\",\n",
    "    \"[archive.apache.org/dist/hadoop/common](https://archive.apache.org/dist/hadoop/common/),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"then update `HADOOP_VER` / `HIVE_VER` in cell 1.2.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**`NoSuchMethodError ... Preconditions.checkArgument`** — the guava fix in 1.3 didn't apply.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Re-run 1.3 and confirm it prints \\\"Guava fix applied\\\".\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**`Schema initialization ... already exists`** — re-run cell 1.4; it deletes `metastore_db` first.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Everything worked, then stopped** — your Colab runtime restarted. Re-run Section 1.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Queries are slow** — expected. Each Hive query starts a small local job; a few seconds per\\n\",\n",
    "    \"query is normal here. On a real cluster the same query runs over vastly more data.\\n\"\n",
